    description: "FOUND 2026-10-03 during INV-dulum-kakum-guhus-podas-mirup-lavut-jamob-kakif's sibling-language sweep (branch jgstern-agent/fix/dulum). NOT the same mechanism as INV-dulum -- recorded because it mints the same kind of fabricated boundary.\n\nTHE FACT. In rust.py the `.method()` path looks the METHOD NAME up in `use_aliases`: for a method call `full_scoped_name` is None, and `elif callee_name in use_aliases:` (rust.py:2879) runs with no method-call guard. So a method call whose NAME equals a `use`-imported free function is qualified as that function.\n\nLIVE REPRO (dev b7417402dc):\n\n    use std::fs::write;\n    use std::io::Write as _;\n    fn f(mut buf: Vec<u8>) { buf.write(b\"x\").unwrap(); }   // Vec::write (io::Write)\n    fn g() { write(\"p\", b\"x\").unwrap(); }                  // std::fs::write\n\n  -> calls f -> rust:std::fs:0-0:write   WRONG (an in-memory Vec write, typed as a filesystem write)\n  -> calls g -> rust:std::fs:0-0:write   right\n\nDIRECTION: false positive -- an fs_write-shaped edge (and so a boundary / taint sink) on a method call on an arbitrary receiver. `write`/`read`/`open`/`remove` are exactly the names both the `use`-imported free functions and common trait methods carry. NOT MEASURED. WI-zojis (done) fixed an earlier Rust method-name collision; this is a path it did not cover. `::` path calls are structurally immune to the INV-dulum shadow shape (a `let` cannot be the root of a `::` path)."  # 38b3
    fields: {}  # 38b3
